    "G3_H0_end_group_defect_robustness": {
      "H0": "The EEW<->OHV cross-titration identity (and hence the EEW -> n_bar inversion) is robust to realistic DGEBA end-group defects: <=1% relative residual for up to 5 mol% alpha-glycol and/or chlorohydrin ends.",
      "defect_rows_breaching_1pct_bar": 12,
      "defect_rows_tested_at_or_above_1molpct": 16,
      "dose_response_monotone_in_glycol_load": true,
      "interpretation": "Two distinct results. (i) The invariance proved in G2 is an IDEAL-SERIES property only: it survives arbitrary polydispersity but NOT epoxide-consuming end groups, which add hydroxyl and remove epoxide simultaneously and so move EEW and OHV off the one-parameter curve in different directions. The residual is therefore not noise - it is a zero-parameter DETECTOR of non-ideal DGEBA, monotone in defect load. (ii) The detector's power is strongly GRADE-DEPENDENT. On low-advancement liquid grades every defect row breaches the bar, because the ideal hydroxyl baseline is small and the two extra OH per hydrolyzed end are a large relative perturbation. On advanced grades the backbone already carries a large ideal OHV, the same defect is a small relative perturbation, and only the heavier loads breach. Relative sensitivity therefore falls by ~17.2x from the liquid to the semisolid grade, so a single fixed relative acceptance bar is NOT a valid QC rule across the DGEBA grade range.",
      "max_nbar_relative_bias": 0.302113,
      "max_residual_relative": 0.410206,
      "passed": true,
      "refutation_basis": "Universal claim refuted by counterexample: 12 of 16 defect rows at >=1 mol% total defect breach the 1% relative bar, worst case 0.4102 relative. The breach is NOT uniform, and the non-uniformity is the second finding.",
      "relative_sensitivity_loss_liquid_to_semisolid_fold": 17.25,
